comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-02T09:41:48.761Z",pi-glm,"Duplicate #134 ownership reconciled, plus a second masked blocker found and fixed. (1) The competing record no longer declares GitHub ownership: its description no longer leads with 'GitHub issue <url>' (the URL reference is preserved in prose), a related dependency now links it to the imported record, and its status stays closed. The imported record keeps the gh: tag and github_url as sole owner. (2) The gated dry-run then refused with '1 conflicting target(s)': one feature record carried gh: tags for issues #191 and #193 as well as PR #189, so two importable issues resolved to a single item. Those two identity tags were removed (the PR #189 provenance tag stays; the relationship is documented in a comment on that record) so the import creates dedicated records for #191 and #193. Measurement — before: failing run https://github.com/unbraind/pm-linear/actions/runs/41297539843 (2026-11-01T10:27Z, 'GitHub issue #134 has multiple existing owners'). After: the same gated dry-run the scheduled workflow runs (pm-github 2026.10.28 as pinned by the reusable workflow) passes against this branch's tracker: fetched 8, planned 8, import 3, update 5, skip 0, provenance-indexed 8, #134 resolves uniquely to the imported record via provenance-tag. The command is linked as a passing test; its pm-test receipt runs in pm's isolated seeded workspace, so the reconciliation numbers here come from the direct dry-run against this checkout. GitHub API budget was checked before measuring (core remaining above the 1500 floor). Guard unchanged; item stays open for orchestrator close after merge."
